   "project": "fieldnotes",
   "reply": "The `delete INDEX` command is in, and `python tools/check.py` now passes: 6 tests, no line-length violations, exit 0.\n\n**Red:** I wrote the tests before any implementation. The first run failed 4 of 6 tests (2 failures, 2 errors):\n- `AttributeError: module 'fieldnotes.store' has no attribute 'delete'`\n- `AssertionError: 2 != 0` and `2 != 1`, because the CLI rejected `delete` with a usage error.\n\n**Green:**\n- `fieldnotes/store.py`: new `delete(index)` removes and returns the note at that 1-based index. It raises `IndexError(\"no note at index N\")` for 0, negative or too-large values and leaves the file untouched.\n- `fieldnotes/cli.py`: `delete INDEX` prints `deleted: <text>` and exits 0. An out-of-range index prints the error to stderr and exits 1. A missing or non-numeric index gets the usage message and exit 2. I also moved the usage string into one `USAGE` constant that now mentions `delete`.\n- Tests: two in `tests/test_store.py` and a new `tests/test_cli.py` with three.\n\nTwo things to know:\n- **Index base:** I chose 1-based indexes. `list` doesn't print indexes yet, so you have to count lines to pick one. Numbering the `list` output would fix that, but it changes existing output, so I left it alone. Say if you want it.\n- **Scratch notes:** `tools/check.py` requires `NOTES_HOME`, so I ran it against a temp directory and didn't touch real notes.",
